---

<Base title="16. LINQ vs array methods — C# vs TypeScript" description="16. LINQ vs array methods: side-by-side C# and TypeScript examples." slug="linq">
  <div slot="header" id="top">
    <p><small><a href="/">C# vs TS</a> / 16. LINQ vs array methods</small></p>
    <h1>16. LINQ vs array methods</h1>
    <p><strong>How to read:</strong> left is C#, middle is TypeScript, right is blank Mystery (yours). Narrow screens stack.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="linq">
  
  <h2>16. LINQ vs array methods</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>var names = users
  .Where(u =&gt; u.Age &gt;= 18)
  .OrderBy(u =&gt; u.Name)
  .Select(u =&gt; u.Name)
  .ToList();

// query syntax (no TS equivalent)
var q = from u in users
        where u.Age &gt;= 18
        select u.Name;

int total = users.Sum(u =&gt; u.Age);
bool anyAdmin = users.Any(u =&gt; u.Role == "admin");
var dict = users.ToDictionary(u =&gt; u.Id);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>const names = users
  .filter((u) =&gt; u.age &gt;= 18)
  .sort((a, b) =&gt; a.name.localeCompare(b.name))
  .map((u) =&gt; u.name);
// no query syntax — chain methods

const total = users.reduce((s, u) =&gt; s + u.age, 0);
const anyAdmin = users.some((u) =&gt; u.role === "admin");
const allActive = users.every((u) =&gt; u.status === "active");
const dict = Object.fromEntries(users.map((u) =&gt; [u.id, u]));
const dictMap = new Map(users.map((u) =&gt; [u.id, u] as const));</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>C# LINQ</th><th>TS equivalent</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Where</code></td><td><code>filter</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Select</code></td><td><code>map</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SelectMany</code></td><td><code>flatMap</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OrderBy/ThenBy</code></td><td><code>sort/toSorted</code> with comparator</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>First/FirstOrDefault</code></td><td><code>find</code> (<code>T | undefined</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Any/All</code></td><td><code>some/every</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Aggregate/Sum</code></td><td><code>reduce</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Distinct</code></td><td><code>[...new Set(arr)]</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Chunk/GroupBy</code></td><td>manual or <code>Object.groupBy</code> (ES2024)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Lazy vs eager — performance deep dive</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>// LINQ is lazy — nothing runs until enumerated
IEnumerable&lt;int&gt; q = nums.Where(n =&gt; &#123; Console.WriteLine(n); return n &gt; 1; &#125;);
// no output yet
var list = q.ToList(); // now it runs (deferred execution)</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>// Array methods are eager — each step allocates
const out = nums.filter((n) =&gt; &#123; console.log(n); return n &gt; 1; &#125;);
// logs immediately
// For lazy pipelines, use generators:
function* lazyFilter&lt;T&gt;(xs: Iterable&lt;T&gt;, f: (t: T) =&gt; boolean): Generator&lt;T&gt; &#123;
  for (const x of xs) if (f(x)) yield x;
&#125;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>TS <code>sort</code> mutates; use <code>toSorted/toReversed/toSpliced</code> for LINQ-like non-mutating behavior.</li>
    <li><code>Object.groupBy</code> is new — check target/lib before using, otherwise reduce into a <code>Map</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  </section>

